Promoting Sexual and Reproductive Health (SRH) and Gender Based Violence (GBV) life-saving activities in Roraima and Amazonas States of Brazil to properly respond to the needs of Venezuelan migrants and refugees.

1) Prevent sexual violence and respond to the needs of survivors by implementing preventive measures at community levels and health facilities, to protect affected populations, particularly women and girls, from sexual violence; training of public health professionals on clinical management of sexual violence by the eLearning platform; and advocate with governments to putting in place confidential and safe spaces within health facilities to receive and provide survivors of sexual violence with appropriate clinical care and referral; 

2) Prevent the transmission of and reduce morbidity and mortality due to HIV and other STIs by conducting outreach activities to promote HIV/AIDS/STI combination prevention (PEP, PrEP) available in public health system, distributing male and female condoms, providing voluntary HIV/STI counselling, testing and inputs, and immediate linkage to health services for AR/STI treatment, with emphasis in pregnant women; 

3) Prevent excess maternal and newborn morbidity and mortality by conducting community based interventions in shelters, settlements, safe spaces and other places with high concentration of migrant population, to promote information about the availability of safe delivery and EmONC services and the importance of seeking care from health facilities, advice to women during pregnancy, childbirth and the postpartum period; ensuring the access to health centers and hospitals 24 hours per day, 7 days per week through a referral system to facilitate transport and communication from the community to Emergency Obstetric Care (EmOC), and comprehensive emergency obstetric and newborn care (CEmONC); donating clinical delivery assistance equipment and; promoting capacity building of public health professionals on clinical management of obstetric emergencies; 

4) Prevent unintended pregnancies by family planning/contraceptive counseling and education, and ensuring availability of a range of long-acting, reversible and short-acting contraceptive methods (including male and female condoms and emergency contraception); providing information and communication materials that emphasizes informed choice and consent, effectiveness, client privacy and confidentiality, equity, and nondiscrimination. 

5) Support the COVID19 response in the states of Roraima and Amazonas, by promoting Communication with Communities (CwC) strategy to deliver comprehensive information about how to prevent and respond to the COVID19 pandemic in a transversal way to other GBV and SRH activities, donating dignity kits with individual and collective sanitation items, and donating PPE to health professionals and beneficiaries, especially for pregnant women. 

6) Strengthen the government institutional capacities on SRH by implementing capacity buildings on IDU insertion, clinical management of rape and sexual violence, and clinical management of obstetric emergencies for public health professionals; 

7) Promote community participation and resilience by building on existing capacities and resources, stablishing partnership with community leaderships and members of the crisis-affected population to support UNFPA in implementing and monitoring essential SRH activities amongst pregnant women, and women of reproductive age in shelters, settlements, and safe spaces.

